WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.050 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:38.790 align:middle line:90%


00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.010 align:middle line:84%
GEORGE CHURCH: Is
there a section?

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.860 align:middle line:84%
No, there are no
sections for a few days.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.770 align:middle line:84%
The sections, both
the weekly sections

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.170 align:middle line:84%
and the extra sections,
will be on the website.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.820 align:middle line:90%
That's your best place to look.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.695 align:middle line:84%
If you have questions after
you've looked at the website,

00:00:55.695 --> 00:00:57.570 align:middle line:84%
then you should contact
your teaching fellow.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.028 align:middle line:84%
But definitely look
at the website.

00:00:59.028 --> 00:01:02.880 align:middle line:84%
It will be updated
daily, and your sections

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.816 align:middle line:84%
will be assigned probably
by Thursday or Friday, OK.

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:13.350 align:middle line:90%


00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.230 align:middle line:90%
OK, welcome back from the break.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.360 align:middle line:84%
As promised, we're
going to discuss

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.350 align:middle line:84%
the innards of computers that
are going to be helping us

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.050 align:middle line:90%
with computational biology.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:31.110 align:middle line:84%
We have a schematic diagram
here, illustrating in part--

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.710 align:middle line:84%
we'll have similar schematic
diagrams for biological systems

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.240 align:middle line:90%
or biochemical systems.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.680 align:middle line:90%
This is one for transistors.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.170 align:middle line:84%
We have these nonlinear
transistor elements here

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.870 align:middle line:84%
with an input voltage and output
voltage in a controlled voltage

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:50.790 align:middle line:84%
VDD, and a ground here, this
little triangle in the lower

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.710 align:middle line:90%
left.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:58.290 align:middle line:84%
These transistors are in this
circuit here that is certainly

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.520 align:middle line:90%
a higher-level description.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.940 align:middle line:84%
It allows you to put down
the more detailed description

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:12.160 align:middle line:84%
of this voltage curve
as a function of time.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.220 align:middle line:84%
So time is the
horizontal axis here,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.980 align:middle line:84%
ranging out to 200
nanoseconds, and this is all

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:22.110 align:middle line:84%
done in a program, a
simulator called Spice.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.570 align:middle line:84%
of this complementary
metal oxide inverter.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.910 align:middle line:84%
And this sort of simulator
is one of the things

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.257 align:middle line:84%
that we will be talking
about in biological systems.

00:02:30.257 --> 00:02:32.340 align:middle line:84%
And it's very useful for
designing these circuits,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.120 align:middle line:84%
and you can see as the
voltage, VDD, goes up

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.890 align:middle line:84%
in this straight blue line
from 0 volts, which is off,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.600 align:middle line:90%
to 5 volts, which is on.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.620 align:middle line:84%
You get this almost all
or none, but certainly

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.080 align:middle line:84%
nonlinear response,
where the output

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.320 align:middle line:84%
voltage on the vertical
axis goes from 5 volts,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.090 align:middle line:90%
on, to 0 volts, off.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.500 align:middle line:90%
It's basically the opposite.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.640 align:middle line:90%
When it's 0, it's 5.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.410 align:middle line:90%
When 5, it's zero.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.570 align:middle line:84%
But in between, you can
see there's this gray zone.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.060 align:middle line:84%
You want to stay away from
this in digital circuitry.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:08.820 align:middle line:84%
You want to stay fully
saturated or basically 0 volts.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:15.630 align:middle line:84%
And then these inverters
can be wired together

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.430 align:middle line:84%
in even higher-level
diagram into what

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.340 align:middle line:84%
are called registers,
which allow you to store

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.190 align:middle line:90%
multi-digit binary numbers.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.540 align:middle line:84%
These can then be
coupled together.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:30.450 align:middle line:84%
So you can take two registers
and add, digit by digit,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.950 align:middle line:90%
the contents of those registers.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.200 align:middle line:90%
That's called an adder.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.320 align:middle line:84%
Adders and a variety of
other higher-level electronic

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.740 align:middle line:84%
components can be put
together and then addressed

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.290 align:middle line:90%
by software called a compiler.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.258 align:middle line:84%
Well, how did you get
this software in there

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:46.050 align:middle line:90%
in the first place?

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.290 align:middle line:84%
We have to toggle
the hardware until it

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.600 align:middle line:84%
gets into a state where
you sort of manually

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.840 align:middle line:84%
get the transistors to be in
the right set of on and off

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.850 align:middle line:90%
voltages, 5 and 0 volts.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.460 align:middle line:84%
Once you get the first
compiler, you can now

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.990 align:middle line:90%
work at a much higher level.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.500 align:middle line:84%
A compiler is
basically something--

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.500 align:middle line:84%
all this code that I've
been showing you so far,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.230 align:middle line:84%
the Perl, the Fortran 77,
the Mathematica, so forth,

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:13.110 align:middle line:84%
those are all things
like, print x above 1.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:16.500 align:middle line:90%
That is like a compiler.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.510 align:middle line:84%
It's a code which
you can type in.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.579 align:middle line:90%
It's almost English.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:24.510 align:middle line:84%
Some of you may say it's not
nearly close enough to English.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.070 align:middle line:84%
But it's much
closer than dealing

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.110 align:middle line:90%
with these little voltages, OK.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.170 align:middle line:84%
And once you have one compiler,
you can make another one.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.930 align:middle line:84%
You can use that
pseudo English to write

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.580 align:middle line:84%
a more complicated
compiler that can

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.347 align:middle line:84%
deal with a even higher-level
language, and then reduce it.

00:04:38.347 --> 00:04:39.930 align:middle line:84%
Then it takes care
of the bookkeeping,

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.750 align:middle line:84%
or reduces it down to
telling the computer what

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.930 align:middle line:90%
voltages to put where and when.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.460 align:middle line:84%
As you go up the
still higher ones,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.920 align:middle line:84%
we have these high level
application programs,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.760 align:middle line:84%
which might have intense
graphics or something

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.650 align:middle line:84%
like that, OK, so
that you really

00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:00.690 align:middle line:84%
get a world view that is
much more in resonance

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:06.120 align:middle line:84%
with our primate visual
and auditory senses.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.770 align:middle line:84%
Now, this idea of self-compiling
and self-assembling

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.840 align:middle line:84%
is very interesting,
very self-referential,

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.110 align:middle line:84%
as many of the things
in this course will be.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:21.540 align:middle line:84%
We have biological components
which have these very

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.210 align:middle line:84%
interesting complementary
surfaces that I talked about

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:29.203 align:middle line:84%
a couple of slides ago, where
the two strands of DNA which

00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:31.620 align:middle line:84%
are not covalently linked--
covalently means that you have

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.520 align:middle line:84%
these strong bonds
along this one ribbon--

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.770 align:middle line:84%
and connected by a
series of stacking

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.090 align:middle line:84%
interactions, where plates, the
sort of planar bases stack up.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:46.230 align:middle line:84%
And they form weak bonds
from one strand to the other

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.220 align:middle line:84%
where the rules I
mentioned before--

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:51.720 align:middle line:84%
now slightly more realistic
symbols here for the GC and AT

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.050 align:middle line:84%
base pairs rather than
just the alphabetics.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.310 align:middle line:90%
But this is still symbols.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:57.810 align:middle line:84%
This is not really
electron density,

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.893 align:middle line:84%
where you're using letters
instead of the electron

00:05:59.893 --> 00:06:03.210 align:middle line:84%
density of nitrogens,
carbons, oxygens, hydrogens.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.630 align:middle line:84%
But these hydrogens make this
hydrogen bond a weak bond,

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.370 align:middle line:84%
and the surfaces
are complementary,

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:11.610 align:middle line:84%
so that if you try
to pair a C with a T,

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.200 align:middle line:90%
you have the wrong spacing.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.750 align:middle line:84%
You have steric
clashes and so on.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:24.390 align:middle line:84%
This process by which
one sequence will not

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.970 align:middle line:84%
make the same sequence
right away-- replication

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.690 align:middle line:84%
does not make an
identical sequence.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.380 align:middle line:84%
It actually makes a
complementary surface.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.370 align:middle line:84%
And then one more cycle, and
you get back to the original,

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.360 align:middle line:84%
just like that
example, I gave before

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.350 align:middle line:84%
of the trinucleotide
going to a hexanucleotide.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.360 align:middle line:84%
And then that helps
the second round.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.840 align:middle line:84%
This is very analogous,
except now these molecules,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.760 align:middle line:84%
instead of being six
nucleotides long,

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.200 align:middle line:84%
they can be hundreds of
millions of nucleotides long.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:59.400 align:middle line:84%
So this introduction
to minimal life,

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.600 align:middle line:84%
all these life have in common
self-assembly, catalysis,

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.750 align:middle line:84%
replication, mutation
and selection.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:09.780 align:middle line:84%
The monomers, meaning
the simple molecules

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.230 align:middle line:84%
taken from the environment,
the environment being defined

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.560 align:middle line:84%
by some boundary-- this could
be a fairly flexible boundary.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.110 align:middle line:84%
It could be a
membranous boundary.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.390 align:middle line:84%
It could just be some
kind of aggregate.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:26.400 align:middle line:84%
The small, simple
monomers, these

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.610 align:middle line:84%
combine to make
complicated polymers.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.670 align:middle line:84%
And then this replication
process continues.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.430 align:middle line:84%
When we go to more
complicated systems,

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.080 align:middle line:90%
now this is a central dogma.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.100 align:middle line:84%
The DNA, the long-term
stable storage,

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.300 align:middle line:84%
is accessed by making
ephemeral RNA, which

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.370 align:middle line:90%
then encodes proteins.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:50.370 align:middle line:84%
And in some systems,
RNA can replicate.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.250 align:middle line:84%
RNA can be
reverse-transcribed to DNA.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.750 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, the AIDS virus
is one of them that does this.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:58.260 align:middle line:84%
DNA can replicate itself, and
certain pathological cases,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:00.810 align:middle line:84%
or actually certain cases,
I should say, of proteins,

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:06.030 align:middle line:84%
can in an autocatalytic
cycle recruit other proteins

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.410 align:middle line:90%
to their particular state.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.720 align:middle line:84%
These are called
prions, and they're

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:12.010 align:middle line:84%
based in Mad Cow disease
and things like that.

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:16.950 align:middle line:84%
But again, you've got a
boundary for the replication,

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:20.850 align:middle line:84%
and simple structures come
in and complicated structures

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:21.570 align:middle line:90%
are generated.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.570 align:middle line:84%
When we talk about
the polymers, we

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:28.230 align:middle line:84%
want to quantitate their
amounts in their interactions.

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:30.540 align:middle line:84%
They initiate,
elongate, terminate.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.230 align:middle line:90%
They fold.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.549 align:middle line:84%
They get modified
in various ways.

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:36.929 align:middle line:84%
So it's not just
simple linear polymers.

00:08:36.929 --> 00:08:38.909 align:middle line:84%
Their position in
space is important,

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:41.070 align:middle line:84%
and they are either
degraded or diluted

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.289 align:middle line:90%
during the replication process.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:44.970 align:middle line:84%
This gets even a
little more complicated

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.220 align:middle line:84%
when we talk about
functional genomics.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.690 align:middle line:84%
Here, we measure
the growth rate.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.570 align:middle line:84%
We measure the concentration
of RNA and proteins.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:56.440 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, their
localization, it's

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:57.690 align:middle line:90%
important to measure that too.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.550 align:middle line:90%
That's called gene expression.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.860 align:middle line:84%
And the interactions
are important to be part

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.270 align:middle line:90%
of this measurement process.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:05.580 align:middle line:84%
These measures and models
that I'm talking about

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:10.990 align:middle line:84%
are how we get to defining
enough about living systems

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:12.010 align:middle line:90%
that we can model.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:16.190 align:middle line:90%
So here is another model.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:18.560 align:middle line:84%
This is a Rorschach
test as you might take.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.360 align:middle line:84%
You go into a psychologist's
office maybe long ago,

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.910 align:middle line:84%
and they'd say, look
at this inkblot.

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:26.905 align:middle line:90%
What does it remind you of?

00:09:26.905 --> 00:09:30.730 align:middle line:84%
You know, bad things your
father did, so forth.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:33.260 align:middle line:84%
Here, the Rorschach test is,
what does this curve remind you

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:33.760 align:middle line:90%
of.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.700 align:middle line:90%


00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:39.358 align:middle line:90%
Give me some hints.

00:09:39.358 --> 00:09:41.143 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Exponential curve.

00:09:41.143 --> 00:09:42.810 align:middle line:84%
GEORGE CHURCH:
Exponential curve, great.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.870 align:middle line:84%
And how do you get-- and
this is like the stock crisis

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:49.350 align:middle line:90%
before a dotcom crash.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:51.328 align:middle line:90%
How do you get this?

00:09:51.328 --> 00:09:53.370 align:middle line:84%
How do you find it in
biology for the biologists,

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.453 align:middle line:84%
or how do you find it
mathematically for the rest?

00:09:55.453 --> 00:09:58.040 align:middle line:90%


00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:58.946 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:58.946 --> 00:10:00.890 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: It kind of reminds
me of human population growth.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.515 align:middle line:84%
GEORGE CHURCH: Human
population growth.

00:10:02.515 --> 00:10:05.360 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's a good
biological example.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:07.770 align:middle line:84%
Maybe, and Malthus
and others have said,

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:08.850 align:middle line:90%
this can't go on forever.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:12.470 align:middle line:90%
But this is not a fact.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:15.890 align:middle line:90%
It's a pretty solid speculation.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:18.860 align:middle line:90%
So how do you get this?

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.240 align:middle line:84%
Well, for those
of you who prefer

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:24.050 align:middle line:84%
to see or expect to see
stocks go down as well as up,

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:27.050 align:middle line:84%
we've got an exponential
decay curve in magenta.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:30.290 align:middle line:84%
It sort of is a reflection of
the exponential growth curve.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:34.370 align:middle line:84%
These are just e to the
kt, or e to the minus--

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.590 align:middle line:90%
or where k is positive.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:40.010 align:middle line:84%
And this is the world's simplest
differentiable equation.

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:44.720 align:middle line:84%
y, the y-axis, is a function
of time, the horizontal axis.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:52.370 align:middle line:84%
And the small changes in y with
small changes in t, time, t,

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:53.600 align:middle line:90%
dydt--

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:57.530 align:middle line:84%
that's sort of the slope
of the y as it goes up,

00:10:57.530 --> 00:10:59.090 align:middle line:90%
the blue exponential curve--

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.340 align:middle line:90%
is related to how much y.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:08.120 align:middle line:84%
The more humans are, the faster
the human race replicates, OK.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:09.890 align:middle line:84%
And it just keeps
getting more and more.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:12.110 align:middle line:84%
And that's why it has
this exponential curve.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:14.860 align:middle line:84%
This is much steeper
than quadratic.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.820 align:middle line:84%
And its origins
are way back here.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:19.370 align:middle line:84%
It's similar to
exponential decay,

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:21.245 align:middle line:84%
as you might get with
radioactive substances.

00:11:21.245 --> 00:11:25.370 align:middle line:90%
It follows a reverse process.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:31.202 align:middle line:84%
And if you integrate this, you
get a very simple integral.

00:11:31.202 --> 00:11:32.660 align:middle line:84%
It's what we've
been talking about.

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.460 align:middle line:90%
It's e to the kt.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:38.330 align:middle line:84%
So it's an exponential
function of time, y, say

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.490 align:middle line:84%
the human population
or your stocks,

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:44.955 align:middle line:84%
where e is this number that we
highlighted before, about 2.7.

00:11:44.955 --> 00:11:47.330 align:middle line:84%
If you're interested in half
life, which sometimes people

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:50.480 align:middle line:84%
are, like radioactive decay
or half-life of replication

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:54.140 align:middle line:84%
of bacteria in a solution,
it's a very simple formula

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:56.330 align:middle line:84%
that gets you from
the rate constant k.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:02.600 align:middle line:84%
This is like a biochemical
rate constant to a half-life.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:04.550 align:middle line:90%
This is growth and decay.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:05.530 align:middle line:90%
So what limits this?

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:09.430 align:middle line:84%
Why doesn't it
just keep going up?

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:13.060 align:middle line:84%
What we've been looking at
is the lower left-hand corner

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:16.930 align:middle line:84%
of this graph in slide 29,
where it goes exponentially

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:19.960 align:middle line:90%
up from close to 0, not quite.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:23.440 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, it will
plateau, or worse yet, it

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:24.700 align:middle line:90%
might come down.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.382 align:middle line:84%
And what causes this plateau
is exhaustion of resources.

00:12:27.382 --> 00:12:29.590 align:middle line:84%
And if you get enough
accumulation of waste products,

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.060 align:middle line:84%
or enough exhaustion
of resources.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:32.860 align:middle line:90%
You can plummet.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:35.140 align:middle line:84%
If you just zoom in on
this little part here,

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:40.360 align:middle line:84%
one way of analyzing it, very
hopefully known to some of you,

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:45.010 align:middle line:84%
is that you take the logarithm
of y and plot it versus t.

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:49.960 align:middle line:84%
So t is linear axis, and the
vertical axis is logarithmic.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.600 align:middle line:84%
Now you get a straight line, at
least for the beginning here.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:56.155 align:middle line:84%
And eventually it will plateau
the same as this one does.

00:12:56.155 --> 00:12:58.780 align:middle line:84%
And that's a way of telling that
you have a simple exponential.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:02.110 align:middle line:84%
If you have e to the t
power, or 2 to the t power,

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:04.563 align:middle line:84%
or anything to the
t power, simple.

00:13:04.563 --> 00:13:05.980 align:middle line:84%
Those are all
simple exponentials,

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:09.590 align:middle line:84%
and they'll give a line
when you take the logarithm.

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:13.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, what does Mathematica
do to help us here?

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:15.820 align:middle line:90%
You set up this equation.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.443 align:middle line:84%
Instead of saying dydt, you
could say, y prime of t.

00:13:18.443 --> 00:13:19.360 align:middle line:90%
That's just shorthand.

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:22.240 align:middle line:84%
It's very commonly
used in calculus.

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.240 align:middle line:84%
y prime of t the
first derivative of t,

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:30.700 align:middle line:84%
of y with respect to t, is
directly proportional to y.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:34.840 align:middle line:84%
That is to say, your slope
of the human race expanding

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:38.330 align:middle line:84%
is directly proportional to
y, the number of humans, OK.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:40.570 align:middle line:84%
And then you're going to
start at time equals 0.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:42.555 align:middle line:90%
We've got one human.

00:13:42.555 --> 00:13:43.930 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's
probably not enough.

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:44.980 align:middle line:90%
Well, OK.

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:45.970 align:middle line:90%
Maybe a bacteria.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:48.520 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:51.130 align:middle line:90%
You have initial conditions, OK.

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:55.600 align:middle line:84%
And then so you
just say solve it.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:57.430 align:middle line:84%
You tell the computer,
so everything

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:00.040 align:middle line:84%
to the right of this
equation is something

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.470 align:middle line:90%
you can type in to Mathematica.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:05.740 align:middle line:84%
Do a differentiable equation
solve of this string

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:07.480 align:middle line:84%
that I typed in
here, which tells you

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.670 align:middle line:84%
the initial conditions
and the formula.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.860 align:middle line:90%
And boom, out comes the out.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:12.880 align:middle line:90%
You didn't type this in.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:15.580 align:middle line:84%
Mathematica came up with
this, e to the t power.

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:16.570 align:middle line:90%
That's pretty cool.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.070 align:middle line:84%
And even though it's the
world's simplest differentiable

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:19.630 align:middle line:90%
equation, it solved it.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:22.030 align:middle line:84%
Try to do that in your
other favorite programming

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:28.720 align:middle line:84%
languages, Excel, or Fortran, or
Perl, or Python or whatever, C.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.730 align:middle line:90%
This is really powerful.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is analytic
or symbolic or formal.

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.970 align:middle line:84%
These are various terms you
would say for this trick.

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:39.160 align:middle line:84%
And as the equations get
more and more complicated,

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:42.185 align:middle line:84%
this becomes more and more
amazing, almost intelligent.

00:14:42.185 --> 00:14:43.810 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, they get
complicated enough

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:47.270 align:middle line:84%
that neither humans nor
Mathematica can solve them.

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:50.650 align:middle line:84%
And so what you do then is
use a numerical approximation

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:56.590 align:middle line:84%
where you take little
steps and you solve it

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:58.960 align:middle line:90%
by numeric approximation.

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:00.580 align:middle line:90%
But you set it up the same way.

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:03.580 align:middle line:84%
You tell it that the
derivative y with respect to t

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.140 align:middle line:90%
is proportional to y.

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:07.960 align:middle line:84%
Or in this case,
proportionality counts as one.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:11.920 align:middle line:84%
Same initial conditions,
one starting bacterium.

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.350 align:middle line:84%
But now you tell it what
interval you want to do this.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:16.850 align:middle line:84%
You don't want it to have to
do these little steps all over,

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:19.690 align:middle line:84%
all negative deposit
of infinity for time.

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.090 align:middle line:84%
You just want to say, I'm
just interested in time from 0

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:26.290 align:middle line:84%
to 3 minutes or hours
or whatever years, OK.

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.240 align:middle line:84%
And then you
evaluate it, and you

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.620 align:middle line:84%
can plot this, which appeals
to the primate visual system,

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:33.580 align:middle line:90%
these plots.

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:35.440 align:middle line:84%
And you'll see lots of
plots in this course.

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:39.680 align:middle line:84%
But now here, y, a function of
t, is this exponential curve.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:41.620 align:middle line:84%
And if we plotted log
of y as a function of t

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:46.363 align:middle line:84%
with this numerical solver,
it would be a straight line.

00:15:46.363 --> 00:15:48.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, I give you some where
it isn't a straight line.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:51.220 align:middle line:84%
These are all logarithmic
on the y-axis,

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:55.105 align:middle line:84%
and they're all linear
on the horizontal axis.

00:15:55.105 --> 00:15:58.780 align:middle line:84%
And they're all time on the
horizontal axis, linear time.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:01.900 align:middle line:84%
And they're more than
simple exponentials.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:03.190 align:middle line:90%
They're going up faster.

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:04.870 align:middle line:84%
Rather than going
out slower, which

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:07.520 align:middle line:84%
we think human population,
bacterial population

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:11.260 align:middle line:84%
and so forth, they'll go
up linear on a log plot

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:13.090 align:middle line:84%
and then flatten
out, these things

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:14.690 align:middle line:84%
are just going
faster and faster.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:15.950 align:middle line:90%
What are these things?

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:17.920 align:middle line:84%
Well, even though your
dotcoms didn't work out,

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:21.730 align:middle line:84%
if you had had a stock portfolio
in Western European commerce

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:25.060 align:middle line:84%
in the year 1000, you'd
be in really good shape

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:26.380 align:middle line:90%
now in the year 2000.

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:28.060 align:middle line:84%
This is not only
going exponential,

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:30.100 align:middle line:84%
it's going steeper
than exponential.

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:32.575 align:middle line:84%
And we all hope that this
will keep going forever,

00:16:32.575 --> 00:16:35.290 align:middle line:84%
that the gross domestic
product of people

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:37.850 align:middle line:84%
in Western Europe and the
world will just keep going up.

00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:39.100 align:middle line:90%
And this is due to technology.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.720 align:middle line:84%
And technology keeps
reinventing itself.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:44.060 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully, it
can keep doing that.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:45.230 align:middle line:90%
Here's another example .

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:47.620 align:middle line:84%
This is more drilling down
to specific technologies

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:50.225 align:middle line:84%
that have been on
a superexponential

00:16:50.225 --> 00:16:51.100 align:middle line:90%
or hyperexponential--

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:53.230 align:middle line:84%
I don't know quite what
the right term here is--

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:54.200 align:middle line:90%
for a long time.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:57.580 align:middle line:84%
These are greater than
linear, steeper than linear.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:01.000 align:middle line:90%
These are close to quadratic.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.980 align:middle line:84%
And so it's an exponent
of a quadratic.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:07.190 align:middle line:84%
And these are for
transmission rate in pink,

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:12.680 align:middle line:84%
of data from the Morse code
in the 1830s to optical fibers

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:14.030 align:middle line:90%
here in the present.

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:18.829 align:middle line:84%
And then the blue are
digital processing

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:24.859 align:middle line:84%
from the first census in the
1890s to modern computers.

00:17:24.859 --> 00:17:30.230 align:middle line:84%
And this is in instructions
per second per $1,000.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:34.160 align:middle line:84%
Now, this unit, the little
piece of this, these integrated

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:36.320 align:middle line:84%
circuits that Moore's
law refers to from just

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:38.700 align:middle line:84%
the tiny end of this
from 1965 onward,

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:39.950 align:middle line:90%
refers to integrated circuits.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:41.390 align:middle line:84%
And these will run out
of gas pretty soon,

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:42.380 align:middle line:90%
everybody tells us.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:45.470 align:middle line:84%
But this curve may not,
because it goes beyond it.

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:49.700 align:middle line:84%
It predates integrated circuits
and it will post date them.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.452 align:middle line:90%
And who knows where this leads.

00:17:52.452 --> 00:17:53.307 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Question.

00:17:53.307 --> 00:17:54.140 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: Yeah.

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:55.528 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: What's the r squared?

00:17:55.528 --> 00:17:56.570 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: Oh, sorry.

00:17:56.570 --> 00:17:58.790 align:middle line:84%
We'll get to that at
the end of this lecture,

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:00.900 align:middle line:84%
but it's a correlation
coefficient,

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:06.170 align:middle line:84%
which is, it's to what extent
is there a fit between one

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:06.920 align:middle line:90%
curve and another.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:08.480 align:middle line:84%
How well does the
calculated curve

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:12.090 align:middle line:90%
fit the observed data collected?

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:16.400 align:middle line:84%
And so these are around 99,
which is very good correlation.

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:18.290 align:middle line:84%
And it's better than
the linear plot,

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:22.270 align:middle line:84%
but of course, you have
more adjustable parameters.

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:22.770 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:27.480 align:middle line:84%
Another sign of hopefulness
is data are coming in faster.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.770 align:middle line:90%
So our life is getting better.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:32.720 align:middle line:84%
Our computers are
getting faster,

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:36.565 align:middle line:84%
and data is coming in
from the Genome Project.

00:18:36.565 --> 00:18:37.940 align:middle line:84%
And this little
inflection point,

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:40.110 align:middle line:84%
where it was log
linear for a while

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:43.160 align:middle line:84%
and then a new log linear-- so
overall, it's superexponential.

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:45.290 align:middle line:84%
And this is for the
number of base pairs

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:47.540 align:middle line:84%
we can get per dollar,
starting with transfer

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:51.590 align:middle line:84%
RNAs in the late '60s
and ending with who

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:54.860 align:middle line:84%
knows how many human genomes
that we will have by the year

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:58.010 align:middle line:90%
2010.

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:01.393 align:middle line:84%
Now, where does this all
this exponential growth go?

00:19:01.393 --> 00:19:03.560 align:middle line:84%
Some people think we will
be creating computers that

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:05.270 align:middle line:90%
are smarter than we are soon.

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:06.710 align:middle line:90%
What would this require?

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:10.520 align:middle line:84%
Here's a nice back of the
envelope example of systems

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.490 align:middle line:84%
analysis where biology
meets computers.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:16.310 align:middle line:90%
Let's analyze our retina.

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:18.890 align:middle line:84%
All of our retinas are
processing right now,

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:20.870 align:middle line:90%
hopefully.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:30.310 align:middle line:84%
And Hans Moravec simulated
a retina for video imaging

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:32.590 align:middle line:84%
where he did edge
and motion detection,

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:39.370 align:middle line:84%
and it required about a
billion instructions per second

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:41.860 align:middle line:84%
to match the 10 times
per second which

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:43.600 align:middle line:90%
you're updating the retina.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:47.660 align:middle line:84%
The brain is about 100,000
times larger than the retina,

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:50.540 align:middle line:84%
and if this scales linearly,
which is speculative,

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:57.460 align:middle line:84%
then you need a computer that
has about 100 million MIPs

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:04.760 align:middle line:84%
or about 10 to the 14
instructions per second

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:08.330 align:middle line:84%
of compute power, and a
similar number of bytes.

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, back in 1998, that was
still quite a ways away.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:14.480 align:middle line:84%
But here in 2002, the best
supercomputer-- and this site

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:17.570 align:middle line:84%
keeps tract of the top
500 supercomputers.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:20.970 align:middle line:84%
And trust me, your computer
is not on that list.

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:23.510 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, the top
one is within a factor

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:25.520 align:middle line:90%
of 10 of this compute power.

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:29.690 align:middle line:84%
Now probably, Earth's
scientists that own this thing

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:35.240 align:middle line:84%
will not bother to try to see if
it can do ordinary human things

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:36.470 align:middle line:90%
like watching soap operas.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:39.890 align:middle line:90%
But we're in that range.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:43.350 align:middle line:84%
We need to be cognizant
of the possibility.

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:44.510 align:middle line:90%
Here's another model.

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:47.177 align:middle line:84%
I've tried to put it in the same
units we've been talking about,

00:20:47.177 --> 00:20:48.710 align:middle line:90%
this exponential growth.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:50.900 align:middle line:84%
Again, we have the
rate constant k.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:54.260 align:middle line:84%
We have the y, the human or
bacterial population growing

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:56.340 align:middle line:90%
exponentially.

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:02.350 align:middle line:84%
And here now, we try to
model the case where yes,

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:04.840 align:middle line:84%
as you have a great
population size,

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.780 align:middle line:84%
it means greater
growth until it gets

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:12.880 align:middle line:84%
close to the maximum carrying
capacity, the 100%, the 1,

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:14.170 align:middle line:90%
the maximum it can go.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:15.730 align:middle line:90%
And then it will plateau.

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:22.660 align:middle line:90%
So you have a plateauing near 1.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:25.030 align:middle line:84%
And this is called
the logistic map.

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:27.160 align:middle line:84%
It is the basis of that
complexity calculation

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:29.260 align:middle line:90%
we talked about earlier.

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:32.470 align:middle line:84%
And here, the population size
is a function of rate constant,

00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:37.900 align:middle line:84%
and it has both grows
when y is small.

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:40.227 align:middle line:84%
As y scales up, it
goes up exponentially.

00:21:40.227 --> 00:21:42.310 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, as it
approaches a maximum of 1,

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:43.510 align:middle line:90%
it plateaus.

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:46.660 align:middle line:84%
However, if you get greedy, and
you increase your growth rate

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:48.520 align:middle line:90%
beyond, say--

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:52.600 align:middle line:84%
here, it's very, very small,
very ungreedy, just 1.01.

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:55.690 align:middle line:84%
That's like a 1% interest
in your bank account, OK.

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:59.320 align:middle line:84%
But still, you'd grow
exponentially given

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:01.383 align:middle line:90%
enough time.

00:22:01.383 --> 00:22:02.800 align:middle line:84%
However, you get
greedy and say, I

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:05.060 align:middle line:84%
want a 300% return
on my investment.

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:07.060 align:middle line:84%
Well, then you start
getting these little cycles

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:10.953 align:middle line:84%
of like stock market
going up and down, OK.

00:22:10.953 --> 00:22:12.370 align:middle line:84%
And if you get
really greedy where

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:17.320 align:middle line:84%
you need a 400% improvement
each cycle, you get chaos.

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:20.770 align:middle line:84%
And then you can eventually drop
down very close to 0 and crash,

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:23.770 align:middle line:84%
and the population
can go extinct

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:29.140 align:middle line:84%
because it used up as resources
or made non-optimal use

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:32.260 align:middle line:90%
and maybe toxic side products.

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:34.730 align:middle line:90%
OK, graphs.

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:38.780 align:middle line:90%
We have directed acyclic graphs.

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:42.650 align:middle line:84%
Just as an example, graphs
are made up of nodes.

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:45.050 align:middle line:84%
You can think of these
here as, the nodes

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:47.570 align:middle line:90%
are people or organisms.

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:51.740 align:middle line:84%
And you start with one bacterium
here on the far left-hand side

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:52.910 align:middle line:90%
of slide 35.

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:55.550 align:middle line:90%
And you have a direction.

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:57.830 align:middle line:90%
You can only go forward in time.

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:03.350 align:middle line:84%
So the node is the
bacterium individual,

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:06.860 align:middle line:84%
and the lines
connecting them are

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:09.770 align:middle line:90%
edges in the graph terminology.

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:10.910 align:middle line:90%
And they're directed.

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:12.860 align:middle line:84%
And they can't go in
cycles because you

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:17.870 align:middle line:84%
can't have a daughter
giving rise to a mother, OK.

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:19.385 align:middle line:84%
So this all makes
intuitive sense.

00:23:19.385 --> 00:23:22.430 align:middle line:90%


00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:25.310 align:middle line:84%
But you can use these kind
of graphs for a whole variety

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:26.420 align:middle line:90%
of interesting things.

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:31.400 align:middle line:84%
You have not just the
pedigree we talked about,

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:34.400 align:middle line:84%
but phylogeny in general,
ancient connections

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:35.990 align:middle line:90%
between organisms.

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:39.440 align:middle line:84%
The biopolymer backbone, you
have a simple linear backbone

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:40.970 align:middle line:90%
or a branch backbone.

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:42.830 align:middle line:90%
This can be represented.

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:47.450 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't covalently
cross back on itself.

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:49.970 align:middle line:84%
If you want to know
what's near one another

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:53.615 align:middle line:84%
as this polymer folds up, like
that transfer I showed you

00:23:53.615 --> 00:23:58.910 align:middle line:84%
in the first slides, those
contacts are indicated.

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:02.460 align:middle line:84%
Now you start getting cycles,
because A can contact B, B, C,

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:04.350 align:middle line:90%
D, back to A again.

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:07.760 align:middle line:84%
You get cycles in a
three-dimensional structure.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:09.710 align:middle line:84%
You get cycles in a
regulatory network.

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:12.650 align:middle line:84%
You can have, in order
to maintain homeostasis

00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:17.210 align:middle line:84%
in your body, A can regulate
B. B can regulate C, and back

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:18.930 align:middle line:90%
to A again.

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:23.000 align:middle line:90%
But that's all directed.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:27.380 align:middle line:84%
There are system models that
we and others will study.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:29.000 align:middle line:90%
They have in common--

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:31.430 align:middle line:84%
this is slide 37 on
the left-hand side--

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:33.920 align:middle line:90%
the system models.

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:36.755 align:middle line:84%
And they've been chosen mainly
because in the pre-genomic era,

00:24:36.755 --> 00:24:38.210 align:middle line:84%
it was very hard
to get data sets.

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:40.400 align:middle line:84%
Certain systems were
just technically easier

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:44.870 align:middle line:84%
to get large data sets,
genetic or biochemical.

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:48.560 align:middle line:84%
These include E. Coli,
going toward food and away

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:50.000 align:middle line:90%
from toxins.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.150 align:middle line:84%
Red blood cell is a
nice metabolic system

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:55.520 align:middle line:84%
because it doesn't have
any polymer synthesis.

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:56.480 align:middle line:90%
Makes it simpler.

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:59.390 align:middle line:84%
Cell division cycle is really
key for understanding pathogen

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:01.400 align:middle line:90%
replication, cancer.

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:09.065 align:middle line:84%
Circadian rhythm, a
huge number, many if not

00:25:09.065 --> 00:25:13.190 align:middle line:84%
possibly all organisms,
have some circadian rhythms

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:18.740 align:middle line:84%
that keep their biochemistry
optimal, and keep us hopefully

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:23.940 align:middle line:84%
awake, right now,
anyway, until it's time.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:24.440 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:26.810 align:middle line:84%
Plasmid DNA replication
is an example

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:28.020 align:middle line:90%
of single-molecule precision.

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:30.950 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about
the DNA single molecules

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:31.790 align:middle line:90%
in just a moment.

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:35.510 align:middle line:90%


00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:38.170 align:middle line:84%
So that's where we're
aiming right now,

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.960 align:middle line:84%
is from graphs and pedigrees
down to the single molecules

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:44.260 align:middle line:90%
that allow replication to work.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:47.950 align:middle line:84%
This replication is achieved
by interconnected machines

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.700 align:middle line:90%
they are somewhat modular.

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:52.120 align:middle line:84%
"Modular" is also
a computer term

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:54.130 align:middle line:84%
where you try to put
code that works together

00:25:54.130 --> 00:25:57.760 align:middle line:84%
into something that's defined
spatially and functionally.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:03.220 align:middle line:84%
So you have these little
modules that replicate the DNA,

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:04.630 align:middle line:90%
make RNA from it.

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:06.670 align:middle line:84%
A different module does
the protein synthesis.

00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:08.543 align:middle line:84%
There is some interconnection
between these.

00:26:08.543 --> 00:26:10.960 align:middle line:84%
This will be discussed. these
kind of complicated machines

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:14.110 align:middle line:84%
that biologists love
to simplify in diagrams

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:19.010 align:middle line:84%
will be described in
more detail next week.

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:21.850 align:middle line:84%
But the idea is
this idea of modules

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:24.460 align:middle line:84%
versus extensively
coupled networks.

00:26:24.460 --> 00:26:28.560 align:middle line:84%
This is how we get
the replication.

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:32.400 align:middle line:84%
The way we analyze the
replication is somewhere here

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:36.540 align:middle line:84%
in the middle, where
I've had a scale here

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:43.770 align:middle line:84%
that goes from high resolution,
very accurate descriptions

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:46.770 align:middle line:84%
of physical processes
sort of in the nanometer

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:51.090 align:middle line:84%
femtosecond range on the far
right-hand side of slide 40,

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:54.030 align:middle line:84%
to things that are
very long timescale,

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:56.460 align:middle line:84%
very large scale
sort of kilometers,

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:59.160 align:middle line:84%
years that happen in
population dynamics,

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:02.513 align:middle line:84%
sometimes global
population dynamics.

00:27:02.513 --> 00:27:04.680 align:middle line:84%
You should understand that
all of these models we'll

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:06.900 align:middle line:84%
be talking about
are approximations.

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:09.840 align:middle line:84%
As we go down the
scale, it gets more

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:17.520 align:middle line:84%
and more computable to compute
more and more complicated

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:19.830 align:middle line:84%
things, but at the
cost of greater

00:27:19.830 --> 00:27:22.350 align:middle line:90%
and greater approximations.

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:26.700 align:middle line:84%
Even the molecular mechanics
that we use in conjunction

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:29.700 align:middle line:84%
with crystallographic
diffraction data

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:33.733 align:middle line:84%
is amazing
computational chemistry,

00:27:33.733 --> 00:27:36.150 align:middle line:84%
but it's a great approximation
of quantum mechanics, which

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:38.692 align:middle line:84%
in turn is an approximation of
quantum electrodynamics, which

00:27:38.692 --> 00:27:39.900 align:middle line:90%
itself is an approximation.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:43.140 align:middle line:84%
And all of these things
are very hard to compute

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:48.870 align:middle line:84%
on any even reasonable atomic
system multi-body problem.

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:50.790 align:middle line:84%
The big approximation
for molecular mechanics

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:52.930 align:middle line:84%
is that you have
spherical atoms,

00:27:52.930 --> 00:27:55.800 align:middle line:84%
so you don't have the
distortion of the dipole that

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:58.110 align:middle line:84%
occurs in very useful
bonds, such as the hydrogen

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:01.530 align:middle line:84%
bonds and almost all
non-bond interactions.

00:28:01.530 --> 00:28:03.810 align:middle line:84%
That's poorly
approximated, but it's

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:06.840 align:middle line:84%
the best we have that can be
computed right now with most

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:10.860 align:middle line:84%
computers and even
modestly large molecules.

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:14.040 align:middle line:84%
Then now, as we go
down, we can think of it

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:16.298 align:middle line:84%
as higher and
higher-level abstraction.

00:28:16.298 --> 00:28:18.090 align:middle line:84%
Just like high-level
programming languages,

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.310 align:middle line:84%
we're now programming
chemical systems

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:21.840 align:middle line:90%
and thinking about them.

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:23.940 align:middle line:84%
Now instead of dealing
with single atoms

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:26.760 align:middle line:84%
in molecular mechanics that
produced the tRNA structure

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:29.580 align:middle line:84%
that I showed you, we now
deal with that whole tRNA

00:28:29.580 --> 00:28:31.000 align:middle line:90%
as a single molecule.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.200 align:middle line:84%
But it's still a great
depth of precision,

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:35.845 align:middle line:84%
because each molecule
has its own life,

00:28:35.845 --> 00:28:37.470 align:middle line:84%
and you track each
one on the computer.

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.410 align:middle line:84%
And that's stochastic
simulation.

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:43.110 align:middle line:84%
The next higher level
beyond that is now,

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:44.610 align:middle line:84%
we don't deal with
single molecules.

00:28:44.610 --> 00:28:46.590 align:middle line:84%
We deal with populations
of molecules,

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:49.387 align:middle line:84%
or we deal with a concentration
as a function of time.

00:28:49.387 --> 00:28:51.720 align:middle line:84%
The ordinary differentiable
equations that we've already

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:55.230 align:middle line:84%
been talking about, like that
exponential growth curve,

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:59.430 align:middle line:84%
is one way of dealing with
concentration of bacteria

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:01.390 align:middle line:90%
as a function of time.

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:02.940 align:middle line:90%
That's appropriate.

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:05.440 align:middle line:84%
There are other cases where we
want to do this optimization.

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:08.880 align:middle line:84%
We want to study how close
to optimal a system is.

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:14.250 align:middle line:84%
One way to study that is with
these economic functions,

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:18.210 align:middle line:84%
these linear programming,
to look at the fluxes.

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:20.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're no longer talking
about concentration and time,

00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:29.100 align:middle line:84%
because we're interested in the
rates through which chemicals

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:33.300 align:middle line:84%
are flowing through a
biological system, where

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:37.800 align:middle line:84%
any particular chemical
concentration is

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:39.090 align:middle line:90%
at a steady-state level.

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:42.027 align:middle line:84%
And that means you have things
going in and things going out,

00:29:42.027 --> 00:29:43.860 align:middle line:84%
but stuff in the middle
is staying the same.

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:47.910 align:middle line:84%
That's a very useful
approximation.

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:48.960 align:middle line:90%
It's used time and again.

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.630 align:middle line:84%
Even though these are dynamic
systems, you can find them.

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:55.110 align:middle line:84%
In a pseudo-steady state, you
can apply these very powerful

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:56.100 align:middle line:90%
computing tools.

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:57.850 align:middle line:84%
And then you can do
computations that

00:29:57.850 --> 00:30:00.540 align:middle line:84%
would be very hard
to do in these more

00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:03.150 align:middle line:90%
precise and complete methods.

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:05.070 align:middle line:84%
And we'll go through
these in much more detail

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:07.560 align:middle line:84%
later on you'll find very
interesting connections

00:30:07.560 --> 00:30:09.930 align:middle line:84%
between the larger-scale
things we're talking about,

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:13.320 align:middle line:84%
where we talk about
stochastics of whole organisms

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:18.174 align:middle line:84%
in big populations, and the
stochastic of single molecules.

00:30:18.174 --> 00:30:18.990 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:20.640 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about
single molecules.

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.560 align:middle line:90%
This is our last topic today.

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:25.860 align:middle line:84%
And each of you do
single-molecule manipulations

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:30.270 align:middle line:84%
on a regular basis,
and your ancestors

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:33.240 align:middle line:84%
have been doing it for $10,000
years without a license,

00:30:33.240 --> 00:30:34.860 align:middle line:90%
without a computer.

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:37.590 align:middle line:84%
And they've been doing
a pretty good job of it.

00:30:37.590 --> 00:30:40.110 align:middle line:84%
They've taken this little
weedlike thing, teosinte,

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:43.080 align:middle line:84%
and turned it into this corn
that would make the 4th of July

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:44.130 align:middle line:90%
quite proud.

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:47.950 align:middle line:84%
And dogs, who knows what
their ancestors looked like.

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:54.900 align:middle line:84%
But right now, they span about
three logarithms of mass.

00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:59.550 align:middle line:84%
And this was all done with the
awesome power of single DNA

00:30:59.550 --> 00:31:04.380 align:middle line:84%
molecule technology,
crosses basically.

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:07.090 align:middle line:84%
And what happens in each
of these cells in your body

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:09.450 align:middle line:84%
if all goes well
is you start out

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:13.800 align:middle line:90%
with one chromosome of interest.

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:17.010 align:middle line:84%
And it divides, and
then the cell divides.

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.260 align:middle line:84%
And that chromosome-- we'll
forget about all the other

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:21.060 align:middle line:84%
chromosomes in
there for a moment--

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:24.960 align:middle line:84%
that chromosome has a
choice of when it divides,

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:28.790 align:middle line:84%
it can go one each into
each of the daughter cells.

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:31.310 align:middle line:84%
Or both chromosomes
can hang out together,

00:31:31.310 --> 00:31:33.060 align:middle line:84%
since they're all
tangled or something,

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:34.477 align:middle line:84%
and then one of
the daughter cells

00:31:34.477 --> 00:31:36.380 align:middle line:84%
doesn't get any copy
of that chromosome.

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:38.150 align:middle line:84%
That obviously is
not a good thing.

00:31:38.150 --> 00:31:40.010 align:middle line:84%
Even if two copies
of the chromosome

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:43.220 align:middle line:84%
is OK, if you can tolerate
that extra dosage,

00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:47.410 align:middle line:84%
you certainly can't
tolerate zero chromosomes.

00:31:47.410 --> 00:31:49.180 align:middle line:84%
Well, what's the chances
this will happen?

00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:51.000 align:middle line:84%
Well this is really
elementary probability.

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.250 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to ease you into it.

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:55.697 align:middle line:84%
It's that it's
about 50/50 chance.

00:31:55.697 --> 00:31:56.280 align:middle line:90%
These are all.

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:58.590 align:middle line:84%
This is the exhaustive
list of the possibilities.

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:06.630 align:middle line:84%
And about 50% of them
have the wrong dosage.

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:10.020 align:middle line:84%
Well, what if we have a
more realistic situation,

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:12.440 align:middle line:90%
human cells with 46 chromosomes?

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:15.180 align:middle line:84%
What are the odds
that they'll all

00:32:15.180 --> 00:32:19.260 align:middle line:84%
be right, that we'll
get exactly one of each?

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:22.492 align:middle line:84%
We have 23 chromosomes
from mom and 23 from pop.

00:32:22.492 --> 00:32:24.450 align:middle line:84%
What's the odds that this
is going to work out?

00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:26.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, we're going to
take a couple of slides

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:27.450 align:middle line:90%
to get to that answer.

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:30.940 align:middle line:90%


00:32:30.940 --> 00:32:32.730 align:middle line:84%
But first, to
motivate you, this is

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:35.430 align:middle line:84%
extremely important in
a health care sense.

00:32:35.430 --> 00:32:39.420 align:middle line:84%
It's the most common form of
mutation, happens all the time.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:42.870 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, at
every chromosome,

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:50.580 align:middle line:84%
duplication or losses is a
big change in the human state.

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:54.198 align:middle line:84%
And the mildest of all of
the additions or subtractions

00:32:54.198 --> 00:32:55.740 align:middle line:84%
of a chromosome--
here, you just have

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:58.770 align:middle line:84%
three copies of chromosome
21, everything else normal.

00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:03.030 align:middle line:84%
So 1.5 dosage of one of the
smallest human chromosomes

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:04.590 align:middle line:90%
has an enormous impact.

00:33:04.590 --> 00:33:07.770 align:middle line:84%
Most of you have seen someone
with Down syndrome, which

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:12.510 align:middle line:84%
is severe mental retardation,
and heart defects

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:13.765 align:middle line:90%
in various other organs.

00:33:13.765 --> 00:33:14.640 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:16.350 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: Yeah, question.

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:20.220 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: This problem that you
just described, in reality,

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:23.700 align:middle line:84%
though, it's not random,
because there are mechanisms

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:28.977 align:middle line:84%
in the cell that would
bias the tool in order

00:33:28.977 --> 00:33:30.060 align:middle line:90%
not to segregate one cell.

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:30.935 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: Right.

00:33:30.935 --> 00:33:32.880 align:middle line:90%
This is a good point.

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:37.860 align:middle line:84%
This is what I'm setting us up
for, exactly that conclusion.

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:39.150 align:middle line:90%
It cannot be random.

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:44.040 align:middle line:84%
Single molecules are
subject to stochastics.

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:47.220 align:middle line:84%
And so to overcome that
stochastic process that

00:33:47.220 --> 00:33:49.650 align:middle line:84%
should be random, you
have to have machines

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:53.333 align:middle line:84%
that involve multiple
molecules, because only

00:33:53.333 --> 00:33:54.750 align:middle line:84%
through multiple
molecules can you

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:57.400 align:middle line:84%
get the statistics to
overcome the single molecule.

00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:59.460 align:middle line:90%
And that's quite a trick.

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:01.260 align:middle line:84%
You can't just casually
say, oh, there's

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:05.100 align:middle line:84%
some machine in there that
takes care of this, OK.

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:06.850 align:middle line:84%
Just to expand this
a little bit more,

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:08.520 align:middle line:84%
we know that
certainly, the DNA is

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:11.409 align:middle line:84%
the case where the single
molecule is always a problem.

00:34:11.409 --> 00:34:15.370 align:middle line:84%
It has to be aided by molecular
machines where you use energy.

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:17.940 align:middle line:84%
You expend energy in order to
make sure the DNA molecules

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:18.500 align:middle line:90%
work.

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:20.250 align:middle line:84%
We'll get back to that
calculation of what

00:34:20.250 --> 00:34:22.590 align:middle line:84%
the odds are at random,
but I should say also,

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:26.940 align:middle line:84%
RNAs in many systems appear
to be, on average-- remember,

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:28.440 align:middle line:84%
it's the population
average to be

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.090 align:middle line:90%
close to 1 molecule per cell.

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:31.949 align:middle line:84%
They're produce in
bursts, following

00:34:31.949 --> 00:34:34.199 align:middle line:84%
stochastic bursts
of RNA where you get

00:34:34.199 --> 00:34:36.239 align:middle line:90%
transcription factors binding.

00:34:36.239 --> 00:34:39.090 align:middle line:84%
And then that burst of RNA
produces even bigger bursts

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:40.260 align:middle line:90%
of proteins.

00:34:40.260 --> 00:34:42.900 align:middle line:84%
But on average, it comes out
to be a very small number,

00:34:42.900 --> 00:34:44.639 align:middle line:90%
because the proteins preserve.

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:47.250 align:middle line:84%
They persist through
many cell divisions

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:50.909 align:middle line:84%
while the RNAs maybe can
turn over more rapidly.

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:54.389 align:middle line:84%
To get back to that question
of how much variation

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:57.300 align:middle line:84%
is tolerable in
biological systems,

00:34:57.300 --> 00:35:01.320 align:middle line:84%
here's the very beginnings
of your statistics.

00:35:01.320 --> 00:35:03.450 align:middle line:84%
Some of you may have
this hopefully already.

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:05.910 align:middle line:84%
There will be sections
where you can cover this.

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:10.260 align:middle line:84%
But here are some of the really,
really useful, easy statistics.

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:12.580 align:middle line:84%
What do we want to know
about a distribution?

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:15.990 align:middle line:84%
Making the fewest
assumptions for now,

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:20.160 align:middle line:84%
we want to know its mean,
its arithmetic average.

00:35:20.160 --> 00:35:23.190 align:middle line:84%
What is the average number
of chromosomes in a cell?

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:26.760 align:middle line:84%
If they're supposed to be 1, how
close to the average are they?

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:27.675 align:middle line:90%
That's the variance.

00:35:27.675 --> 00:35:30.660 align:middle line:90%


00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:33.000 align:middle line:84%
To get the arithmetic
mean, you basically

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.980 align:middle line:84%
add up all the
numbers and divide

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:37.530 align:middle line:90%
by the number that you counted.

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:42.460 align:middle line:84%
Add up the values
which are the axes.

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:44.580 align:middle line:84%
And so here you
take a weighted sum.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:48.210 align:middle line:84%
The sigma means the sum
of the x values weighted

00:35:48.210 --> 00:35:50.850 align:middle line:84%
by the frequency, f
of x, the frequency

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:53.580 align:middle line:84%
that they occur in the
sample that you take.

00:35:53.580 --> 00:35:55.830 align:middle line:90%
Here, r for the mean is just 1.

00:35:55.830 --> 00:36:02.250 align:middle line:84%
It's taking the first
moment to get the mean.

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:07.830 align:middle line:84%
The analogous thing is, now
you correct all the values

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:10.230 align:middle line:84%
that you're measuring,
these variables,

00:36:10.230 --> 00:36:11.790 align:middle line:90%
the number of chromosomes, say.

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:13.360 align:middle line:90%
And you subtract the mean.

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:18.180 align:middle line:84%
So now the mean for this x
minus mu is now effectively 0.

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:21.000 align:middle line:84%
The mean is 0, and you
want to ask, how far from 0

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.040 align:middle line:90%
do you deviate.

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:25.770 align:middle line:84%
For chromosomes, you want that
deviation to be very small.

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:28.410 align:middle line:84%
You want the variance
to be very small.

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.270 align:middle line:84%
And it's just the
sum of the squares.

00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:34.410 align:middle line:84%
We want to take the squares
because if it deviates either

00:36:34.410 --> 00:36:37.920 align:middle line:84%
more or less, it's
still a tragedy,

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:39.800 align:middle line:84%
and you want to
keep tract of that.

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:40.800 align:middle line:90%
So these are two things.

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:42.600 align:middle line:84%
They don't make any
assumption about what kind

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:43.380 align:middle line:90%
of distribution.

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:45.300 align:middle line:84%
The distribution
can be anything.

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:46.920 align:middle line:84%
You can calculate
the arithmetic mean

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:51.180 align:middle line:90%
and the standard deviation.

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:53.670 align:middle line:84%
Another useful
concept that starts

00:36:53.670 --> 00:36:55.560 align:middle line:84%
to make more assumptions
in interpretation

00:36:55.560 --> 00:36:59.880 align:middle line:84%
is, now you have two
variables, say x and y.

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:01.757 align:middle line:84%
And you want to ask,
do they covariate.

00:37:01.757 --> 00:37:03.090 align:middle line:90%
Are they related to one another?

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:07.530 align:middle line:84%
When you do two
different experiments,

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:09.955 align:middle line:84%
you want to ask whether
they're giving similar results.

00:37:09.955 --> 00:37:12.330 align:middle line:84%
If they're two completely
different kinds of experiments,

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:14.913 align:middle line:84%
you might want to know whether
they're reinforcing each other.

00:37:14.913 --> 00:37:16.770 align:middle line:84%
You want to know whether
they're redundant.

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:18.930 align:middle line:84%
If you're observing
two biological facts,

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:21.370 align:middle line:84%
you don't know whether they
are related to one another.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:23.100 align:middle line:90%
It's a discovery if they covary.

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:24.090 align:middle line:90%
That's what this means.

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:27.060 align:middle line:84%
Covariance is, again, using
this concept of expectation.

00:37:27.060 --> 00:37:32.370 align:middle line:84%
The sum of the x's corrected,
so that you subtract

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:34.980 align:middle line:84%
the means, their
averages, and divide

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:38.380 align:middle line:84%
by their standard deviations,
square root of the variance.

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:42.210 align:middle line:84%
So you basically end up with a
mean of 0 and a variance of 1.

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:47.340 align:middle line:84%
And then whenever these
normalize, when x goes up

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:52.060 align:middle line:84%
and y goes up, the product
will be reflected in this sum.

00:37:52.060 --> 00:37:56.450 align:middle line:84%
So now this has an
interesting property

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:59.150 align:middle line:84%
that when x and y are
independent, unrelated,

00:37:59.150 --> 00:38:04.060 align:middle line:84%
then the C or the Pearson
correlation coefficient is 0.

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:07.130 align:middle line:84%
However, the
reverse is not true.

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:10.370 align:middle line:84%
If C is 0, it does not
imply that the x and y

00:38:10.370 --> 00:38:11.780 align:middle line:90%
are independent.

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:15.260 align:middle line:84%
An example, a simple example, is
the curve, the quadratic curve,

00:38:15.260 --> 00:38:16.550 align:middle line:90%
y equals x squared.

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:20.420 align:middle line:84%
Here, they are completely
related to one another,

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:22.670 align:middle line:84%
but they give a correlation
coefficient of 0.

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:25.190 align:middle line:84%
That's because this is a
linear correlation coefficient.

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:27.890 align:middle line:84%
The model that you're testing
is that they are linear related.

00:38:27.890 --> 00:38:29.960 align:middle line:84%
They are either
positively correlated,

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:34.130 align:middle line:84%
which means the extreme case
of C will be equal to 1,

00:38:34.130 --> 00:38:38.120 align:middle line:84%
or negatively correlated
C is equal to minus 1.

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:40.130 align:middle line:84%
You can plug this
little formula here,

00:38:40.130 --> 00:38:42.260 align:middle line:84%
handy-dandy practical
form you can plug in

00:38:42.260 --> 00:38:43.790 align:middle line:90%
to calculate probabilities.

00:38:43.790 --> 00:38:45.770 align:middle line:90%
And this is an Excel formula.

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:50.210 align:middle line:84%
The probability that a
correlation is far from 0,

00:38:50.210 --> 00:38:54.380 align:middle line:84%
that's dependent upon the
sample size where you sampled

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:55.760 align:middle line:90%
different x's and y's.

00:38:55.760 --> 00:39:01.010 align:middle line:84%
You know, it could be head
size and weight, or length

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:02.660 align:middle line:90%
and weight, and so forth.

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:05.330 align:middle line:84%
If they're correlated,
then this probability

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:09.210 align:middle line:84%
will be significant if your
sample size is large enough.

00:39:09.210 --> 00:39:11.330 align:middle line:84%
There are some very
practical things.

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:15.950 align:middle line:84%
And now let's put these in the
context of a particular class

00:39:15.950 --> 00:39:16.650 align:middle line:90%
of distribution.

00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:18.322 align:middle line:84%
Now, most of those
did not require

00:39:18.322 --> 00:39:20.030 align:middle line:84%
that we state what
kind of distributions,

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:21.980 align:middle line:84%
but there's a big
interesting set which

00:39:21.980 --> 00:39:25.310 align:middle line:90%
are roughly bell-shaped curves.

00:39:25.310 --> 00:39:29.300 align:middle line:84%
And I've rigged this so that
these three wildly different

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:34.280 align:middle line:84%
types of distribution happen
to give similar curves.

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:36.885 align:middle line:84%
And you see in the next
couple slides how I rigged it,

00:39:36.885 --> 00:39:38.760 align:middle line:84%
but basically, this is
a normal distribution,

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.080 align:middle line:84%
the Poisson distribution
and the binomial.

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:44.630 align:middle line:84%
The binomial has
a limited range.

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:47.730 align:middle line:84%
n goes from 0 to
40 in this case.

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:50.030 align:middle line:90%
It has a maximum n of 40.

00:39:50.030 --> 00:39:56.810 align:middle line:90%


00:39:56.810 --> 00:40:02.360 align:middle line:84%
The Poisson has a mean
which in this case is 20.

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:04.640 align:middle line:84%
The normal distribution
has a mean that's similar.

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:09.770 align:middle line:84%
The normal distribution
can have any range,

00:40:09.770 --> 00:40:10.940 align:middle line:90%
the standard deviation.

00:40:10.940 --> 00:40:17.630 align:middle line:84%
And this is set here to be,
the standard deviation to be,

00:40:17.630 --> 00:40:20.360 align:middle line:84%
the square root of the mean
of the Poisson distribution.

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:24.140 align:middle line:84%
That's how you can rig these
to be similar to one another.

00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:27.290 align:middle line:90%


00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:28.790 align:middle line:84%
I think time is
not going to permit

00:40:28.790 --> 00:40:32.990 align:middle line:84%
me to go through
all this in detail,

00:40:32.990 --> 00:40:35.900 align:middle line:84%
but you will cover these
in your statistics sections

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:37.310 align:middle line:90%
if you don't already know it.

00:40:37.310 --> 00:40:40.160 align:middle line:84%
But suffice it to say that
the binomial distribution is

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:40.955 align:middle line:90%
limit x.

00:40:40.955 --> 00:40:43.910 align:middle line:84%
It Has to be an
integer, and the integer

00:40:43.910 --> 00:40:46.160 align:middle line:90%
is limited to going from 0 to n.

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:49.040 align:middle line:84%
This distinguishes it from a
Poisson distribution, where

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:53.570 align:middle line:84%
it goes from 0 to infinity,
and the normal curve,

00:40:53.570 --> 00:40:56.420 align:middle line:84%
where it can go from negative
infinity to positive infinity.

00:40:56.420 --> 00:40:59.300 align:middle line:84%
Binomial and Poisson
are discrete.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:03.890 align:middle line:84%
They happen at integers while
the Gaussians are continuous.

00:41:03.890 --> 00:41:06.920 align:middle line:90%


00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:12.320 align:middle line:84%
The way you calculate
this is, the probability

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:17.990 align:middle line:84%
of an individual n
happening has probability p,

00:41:17.990 --> 00:41:21.650 align:middle line:84%
so say 0.01 in the
previous slide.

00:41:21.650 --> 00:41:26.660 align:middle line:84%
And then getting
exactly x of those

00:41:26.660 --> 00:41:30.260 align:middle line:84%
is p to the x power as
a first approximation.

00:41:30.260 --> 00:41:34.520 align:middle line:84%
But there are actually two cases
here, hence the name binomial.

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:36.830 align:middle line:84%
If it were multiple cases,
they'd be multinomial.

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:39.950 align:middle line:84%
But the two cases are
basically p and 1 minus p.

00:41:39.950 --> 00:41:41.000 align:middle line:90%
They have to sum to 1.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:42.950 align:middle line:84%
All these probabilities
have to sum to 1.

00:41:42.950 --> 00:41:45.980 align:middle line:84%
So now you have the probability
that you have exactly x,

00:41:45.980 --> 00:41:50.120 align:middle line:84%
and the leftovers
go into 1 minus p.

00:41:50.120 --> 00:41:51.620 align:middle line:84%
But then you also
have to correct

00:41:51.620 --> 00:41:53.030 align:middle line:84%
for the number of
different ways that you

00:41:53.030 --> 00:41:54.950 align:middle line:84%
could get this, the
number of combinations,

00:41:54.950 --> 00:42:00.380 align:middle line:84%
which is the total number
of possibilities choosing

00:42:00.380 --> 00:42:01.610 align:middle line:90%
x at a time.

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:05.120 align:middle line:84%
And then again, the
leftover is n minus x.

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:07.977 align:middle line:84%
And this is n factorial
over x factorial times

00:42:07.977 --> 00:42:08.810 align:middle line:90%
n minus x factorial.

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:10.310 align:middle line:84%
This is the number
of combinations.

00:42:10.310 --> 00:42:12.590 align:middle line:84%
And so now the binomial
distribution is this,

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:16.370 align:middle line:84%
and the sum of all the
terms here has to add to 1.

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:20.810 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the properties
of probability distribution,

00:42:20.810 --> 00:42:23.570 align:middle line:84%
is if you think of
all the possibilities,

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:25.700 align:middle line:90%
they add up to 1.

00:42:25.700 --> 00:42:28.730 align:middle line:84%
So the sum of the binomial
distribution of all the x's is

00:42:28.730 --> 00:42:30.050 align:middle line:90%
1.

00:42:30.050 --> 00:42:34.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, just to remind you
that computers are fallible,

00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:36.590 align:middle line:84%
here's what, when you
take a fairly-- you know,

00:42:36.590 --> 00:42:41.270 align:middle line:84%
x is equal to exactly 300
taken from a population of 700.

00:42:41.270 --> 00:42:45.455 align:middle line:84%
A probability of 1
for a unit event.

00:42:45.455 --> 00:42:47.660 align:middle line:84%
The probability of
getting exactly 350

00:42:47.660 --> 00:42:52.100 align:middle line:84%
from that kind of bell-shaped
curve is very small, but not 0.

00:42:52.100 --> 00:42:58.030 align:middle line:84%
And Mathematica gets it right,
and Excels gueses it at 0.

00:42:58.030 --> 00:42:59.638 align:middle line:90%
Good guess, but wrong.

00:42:59.638 --> 00:43:02.820 align:middle line:90%


00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:09.130 align:middle line:84%
Poisson, you now can and must
go out to positive infinity.

00:43:09.130 --> 00:43:14.380 align:middle line:84%
And there, you often will
make the approximation

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:17.510 align:middle line:84%
that for large n and small
probability in the binomial

00:43:17.510 --> 00:43:20.410 align:middle line:84%
we've been talking about-- n
is the total number of objects

00:43:20.410 --> 00:43:22.300 align:middle line:84%
you're looking at,
you're choosing x from.

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:25.270 align:middle line:84%
And t is the unit
probability of each of those.

00:43:25.270 --> 00:43:29.350 align:middle line:84%
The mean is now
approximately n times p,

00:43:29.350 --> 00:43:32.560 align:middle line:84%
and the binomial and
Poisson are very similar.

00:43:32.560 --> 00:43:34.585 align:middle line:84%
That's why they look
similar in that plot.

00:43:34.585 --> 00:43:38.050 align:middle line:90%


00:43:38.050 --> 00:43:41.080 align:middle line:84%
And here's some practical magic
you can do with the Poisson.

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:43.300 align:middle line:84%
If you have a library
of [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:46.120 align:middle line:84%
or combinatorial chemistry, or
genomic clones and so forth,

00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:49.540 align:middle line:84%
and x is a number
of hits, and you

00:43:49.540 --> 00:43:55.690 align:middle line:84%
want that to be greater than
0 so your thesis can proceed,

00:43:55.690 --> 00:44:01.510 align:middle line:84%
you want the 0 hit term to be
very small, e to the minus mu.

00:44:01.510 --> 00:44:04.900 align:middle line:84%
So you want the
mean to be greater

00:44:04.900 --> 00:44:07.030 align:middle line:90%
than 1 or 2 or maybe even--

00:44:07.030 --> 00:44:08.852 align:middle line:84%
if the mean is 10,
the probability

00:44:08.852 --> 00:44:10.810 align:middle line:84%
that for a given experiment
you'll have a 0 hit

00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:12.760 align:middle line:90%
is very small.

00:44:12.760 --> 00:44:17.180 align:middle line:84%
And you can estimate this from
the number and 1 2 and 3 hits

00:44:17.180 --> 00:44:17.680 align:middle line:90%
you get.

00:44:17.680 --> 00:44:19.510 align:middle line:84%
You can estimate
the 0 hit, and you

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:22.510 align:middle line:84%
can estimate whether it
actually fits a Poisson or not.

00:44:22.510 --> 00:44:25.128 align:middle line:84%
The final of this
trio is the normal.

00:44:25.128 --> 00:44:27.420 align:middle line:84%
Now you go from negative
infinity to positive infinity.

00:44:27.420 --> 00:44:30.880 align:middle line:84%
It's not just 0 to
n or 0 to infinity.

00:44:30.880 --> 00:44:32.200 align:middle line:90%
And it's continuous.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:34.220 align:middle line:90%
That means everything takes a p.

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:37.870 align:middle line:84%
So now instead of summing up
to 1, you integrate up to 1,

00:44:37.870 --> 00:44:42.880 align:middle line:84%
because now the little delta
x's are infinitely small.

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:46.818 align:middle line:84%
And so now, here's an
exponential of a quadratic,

00:44:46.818 --> 00:44:48.860 align:middle line:84%
just like the ones we were
talking about earlier.

00:44:48.860 --> 00:44:50.985 align:middle line:90%
So this is a negative quadratic.

00:44:50.985 --> 00:44:53.110 align:middle line:84%
And that gives you a nice
little bell-shaped curve.

00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:58.900 align:middle line:84%
And the 2 pi sigma square
root is a normalization,

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:02.260 align:middle line:84%
so it does actually
integrate sum to 1.

00:45:02.260 --> 00:45:04.540 align:middle line:84%
Here, another approximation
sometimes applied

00:45:04.540 --> 00:45:07.070 align:middle line:84%
is when n times p
times q is large,

00:45:07.070 --> 00:45:09.130 align:middle line:84%
the normal is very
similar to the binomial.

00:45:09.130 --> 00:45:14.075 align:middle line:84%
People will abuse this
and use one of these three

00:45:14.075 --> 00:45:15.700 align:middle line:84%
distributions in the
place of the other

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:16.783 align:middle line:90%
when it isn't appropriate.

00:45:16.783 --> 00:45:18.680 align:middle line:84%
And we'll give some
examples as we go.

00:45:18.680 --> 00:45:20.500 align:middle line:90%
So back to this calculation.

00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:22.990 align:middle line:84%
If we apply the binomial
that we had in those previous

00:45:22.990 --> 00:45:25.210 align:middle line:84%
slides-- and I urge you
to do this as an exercise.

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:29.590 align:middle line:84%
It's not on the problem
set, but just do it.

00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:32.770 align:middle line:84%
Just getting some for
any 46 chromosomes,

00:45:32.770 --> 00:45:36.220 align:middle line:84%
you get the right number of
chromosomes, is about 8%.

00:45:36.220 --> 00:45:37.090 align:middle line:90%
That's not too bad.

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:41.770 align:middle line:84%
But it's still fairly lethal,
because you really want exactly

00:45:41.770 --> 00:45:45.340 align:middle line:84%
the correct 46, which is
0.5 to the 46th power, which

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:47.650 align:middle line:84%
is infinitesimally unlikely
to happen at random, which

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:49.930 align:middle line:84%
gets back to the point
made by the audience

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:52.690 align:middle line:90%
here that this is not random.

00:45:52.690 --> 00:45:55.330 align:middle line:84%
But you can't fight the
fact that single molecules

00:45:55.330 --> 00:45:56.530 align:middle line:90%
are based in stochastics.

00:45:56.530 --> 00:45:59.860 align:middle line:84%
So you have to have a
lot of events adding up,

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:03.040 align:middle line:84%
energy being input
to overcome that.

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:04.810 align:middle line:84%
We have selection
that's optimizing

00:46:04.810 --> 00:46:07.120 align:middle line:90%
this over long periods of time.

00:46:07.120 --> 00:46:09.970 align:middle line:84%
We can use random numbers
that underlie this

00:46:09.970 --> 00:46:12.160 align:middle line:84%
for the simulations of
these stochastic events,

00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:14.320 align:middle line:84%
and also for
permutation statistics,

00:46:14.320 --> 00:46:17.200 align:middle line:84%
that when you have some data and
you want to know whether it's

00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:21.790 align:middle line:84%
significant or not, you can
kind of do a Monte Carlo

00:46:21.790 --> 00:46:23.230 align:middle line:90%
simulation of it.

00:46:23.230 --> 00:46:24.700 align:middle line:84%
Here's how you
code it in a couple

00:46:24.700 --> 00:46:29.565 align:middle line:84%
of different languages, Perl,
Excel, Fortran 77, Mathematica.

00:46:29.565 --> 00:46:30.940 align:middle line:84%
Even though you
can't evaluate it

00:46:30.940 --> 00:46:33.880 align:middle line:84%
by looking at these numbers
on the screen, trust me.

00:46:33.880 --> 00:46:35.563 align:middle line:84%
There are bad random
number generators.

00:46:35.563 --> 00:46:36.980 align:middle line:84%
There are random
number generators

00:46:36.980 --> 00:46:38.290 align:middle line:90%
which are not very random, OK.

00:46:38.290 --> 00:46:41.740 align:middle line:90%


00:46:41.740 --> 00:46:45.580 align:middle line:84%
Where they come from is this
remainder operation, operated

00:46:45.580 --> 00:46:46.990 align:middle line:90%
on very special numbers.

00:46:46.990 --> 00:46:48.910 align:middle line:84%
You'll have to look these
up in this reference

00:46:48.910 --> 00:46:50.800 align:middle line:90%
to get a full feeling for it.

00:46:50.800 --> 00:46:54.012 align:middle line:84%
But these are
deterministic formulas

00:46:54.012 --> 00:46:55.220 align:middle line:90%
that give you random numbers.

00:46:55.220 --> 00:46:57.800 align:middle line:84%
It's not really the
same as flipping a coin.

00:46:57.800 --> 00:47:00.000 align:middle line:84%
The computer actually
will give you--

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:01.750 align:middle line:84%
It's the same random
numbers over and over

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:04.210 align:middle line:84%
again unless you do
something very special.

00:47:04.210 --> 00:47:06.970 align:middle line:84%
And typically, these give
a uniform distribution

00:47:06.970 --> 00:47:11.115 align:middle line:84%
between 0 and 1, or
over some integer range.

00:47:11.115 --> 00:47:13.240 align:middle line:84%
And then you can turn it
into a normal distribution

00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:15.070 align:middle line:84%
with this kind of
trick here where

00:47:15.070 --> 00:47:16.540 align:middle line:90%
you make a transformation.

00:47:16.540 --> 00:47:19.030 align:middle line:84%
There's a difference between
a uniform random distribution

00:47:19.030 --> 00:47:20.710 align:middle line:90%
and a bell-shaped one.

00:47:20.710 --> 00:47:23.560 align:middle line:84%
And you can generate both
of them just with this slide

00:47:23.560 --> 00:47:26.170 align:middle line:90%
alone.

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:29.800 align:middle line:84%
So we full cycle back to these
three different bell curves.

00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:33.220 align:middle line:84%
They have very different
properties that can be applied.

00:47:33.220 --> 00:47:36.640 align:middle line:84%
Binomial only when you have
a limited range, Poisson

00:47:36.640 --> 00:47:38.020 align:middle line:90%
when you're positive infinity.

00:47:38.020 --> 00:47:42.430 align:middle line:84%
Normal, negative,
positive and continuous.

00:47:42.430 --> 00:47:45.670 align:middle line:84%
Thank you for
participating in this.

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:48.175 align:middle line:84%
These are the topics
that we covered.

00:47:48.175 --> 00:47:50.860 align:middle line:90%


00:47:50.860 --> 00:47:52.640 align:middle line:90%
See you back here in a week.

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:54.960 align:middle line:84%
Please hand in your
questionnaires,

00:47:54.960 --> 00:47:59.270 align:middle line:84%
and the sections should be
assigned by Thursday or Friday.

00:47:59.270 --> 00:48:00.840 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Thank you.

00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:40.000 align:middle line:90%